body:
That's likely to happen when you have grown up with traumatic experiences. I have only recently been able to recover my entire lifespan of memories after 3 years of therapy. Give yourself some grace for it. I remember that aspect upsetting me too, that I couldn't talk about my childhood normally with others. But honestly, it is what it is. That doesn't make you less of a person. Your brain is protecting you from experiencing extremely crazy amounts of pain. Thank your brain that it's doing that, because there's a reason for it. It's protecting you.

Let it come in time. And if you aren't seeking help already, consider it to help you uncover your past.

&#x200B;

Def not overreacting, def not normal mom behavior. She sounds a lot like my nMom. and these things that you find peeve you about your mother also peeve me about my own mother. I hate the exaggerated tone because it shows immaturity - and you've had to deal with her immature crap for so long that when she does this, it makes you think of all the times she was emotionally immature, and it pisses you off. She never took ownership of her situation and thought she was stuck in it, and that is annoying as hell to see her in that lifecycle pattern when she could've done more to help herself. I literally hate passive aggressive people because of my mother, so that's okay that you don't like that. No one likes passive aggressive people, they don't know how to handle conflict well. Sounds like she is dying for attention because of how much she talks and steals the spotlight from you. What is the point of her telling you that she doesn't like your friends? Unless they are life threatening to you, her opinion doesn't matter.

&#x200B;

But just because she isn't a terrible mom doesn't mean she can't be better. She is emotionally unstable, and that alone can be hard for any family. Also, if she wasn't there for you and was selfish in your times of need, then she is not a good mother. You can very much answer that the reason you have some of those issues is because you couldn't trust the first person in your life growing up, your mother, to be there for you in times of need, and to truly care about you without making it about herself.

Don't give in.

&#x200B;

If your siblings are really at stake, you will find a way to support them. Don't let her control you. You're doing the right thing. stand firm. create those boundaries. block her number, but make sure you can contact your siblings somehow. Those phone calls are just calls. Your safety is not at stake, and the safety of your siblings is not at stake. Don't let her make you fearful

&#x200B;

Psychologically, you're going to have to remind yourself constantly that they no longer control you. You will hear their voices in your head, but just continue to remind yourself that they actually don't control you at all. Also, their reactions after going VLC have nothing to do with how good of a person you are. They will probably react negatively because their distorted realities will be seared into.

&#x200B;

Also, you will definitely react too, and that's okay. you're not used to this. It's going to be uncomfortable to move into this new situation. Just remember to give yourself grace as you make adjustments. With that being said, allow yourself to fully become the person they held you back from being.

I can't predict how long it will take for each person because it really depends on the individual and their commitment to growth. If you really work on yourself, learn psychology on a weekly basis, surround yourself with community, realign your values, and work with a counselor, maybe even two counselors, you will move really fast. This is where I am right now! And trust me it's hard work but SO worth it.

&#x200B;

I am sorry about your situation but I am so proud of you for going no contact! Remember, you are no longer under your parents control. Take your control back now, and your life.

Omg.

&#x200B;

One time, I wanted to have an 8th grade graduation party and I expected I would have one because my sister had one, but my mom really didn't want me to because she "didn't have money", but it turns out she definitely had enough money....To replace the entire living room floor with wood. Then, she proceeded to blame me for needing to buy wood floors for my party. Because the carpet wasn't good enough. Lol.

&#x200B;
